  "worked_solution": "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrices $A=({a}_{ij})$ and $B=({b}_{ij})$ from $P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha ({a}_{ij}-{b}_{ij})=\\alpha {a}_{ij}-\\alpha {b}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha (A-B)=\\alpha A-\\alpha B.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})$ from $P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta {a}_{ij})=(\\alpha \\beta ){a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta A)=(\\alpha \\beta )A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ from $P.$ Then,\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot A=(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\notin P\n$$\n\nbecause $-1+0\u226f0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
